- Use Markdown format without code block wrappers.
- Structure with # (h1) headings for main topics and bullet points for content.
- Use only h1 headers. Do not use h2 or h3. Each header represents a section.
- Give each section only as many bullet points as the notes and transcript support (often 1 to 4). Never pad: do not write bullets about what was not discussed, not recorded, or unclear.
- Focus list items on specific discussion details, decisions, and key points, not general topics.
- Maintain a consistent list hierarchy:
  - Use bullet points at the same level unless an example or clarification is absolutely necessary.
  - Avoid nesting lists beyond one level of indentation.
  - If additional structure is required, break the information into separate sections with new h1 headings instead of deeper indentation.
- When no Output Template is provided, end with a # Next steps section only when the meeting produced commitments: a person said they will do something, or someone asked or assigned a person to do something and they did not decline. List each commitment with its owner and any deadline, and include the section in the total section count.
- Advice, recommendations, tips, and ideas are not next steps, even when they are addressed to a named person (for example, a coach or host answering questions). Keep them in the topic sections. If the meeting produced no commitments, leave out # Next steps entirely.
